  "query_preview": "..For today and Sunday...a low pressure system was \nseen spinning just off the Baja California coast Saturday \nmorning. Over the next 24- to 36-hours the low center will track \ntoward then across Arizona, weakening and filling as it goes. The \nlow will bring with it upward motion and increasing moisture. \nBy Saturday night scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms \nwill develop from south to north across Arizona starting, then \nlast through Sunday. A few limiting factors with this low. First,\nthe air mass across Arizona is very dry and it will take a bit \nfor the depth of the atmosphere to moisten. In addition, the flow \nassociated with this low is weak so very little if any orographic \nlift. Another factor, it will remain mostly cloudy as the low \nmoves across the area with little impact due to daytime heating. \nAs a result, looking at generally light precipitation totals of \n0.10 inches or less through early Monday (higher amounts over the \nhighest mountain peaks perhaps as much as 0.50 inches). In any \ncase, this is a warm system so snow levels are expected to remain \nprimarily above 11,000 feet but can't rule out snow levels briefly\ndipping a bit lower at the onset of precipitation due to \nevaporational cooling. Easterly winds Saturday afternoon will \nbecome light and variable on Sunday as pressure gradients become \nindeterminate. There will be a cooling influence from the light \nprecipitation and clouds with high temperatures dropping by around\n5 degrees on Sunday compared to Saturday.\n\nFor Monday and Tuesday...A strong low pressure system dropping\nsouthward from the Gulf of Alaska will begin to approach Arizona.\nMoisture will continue to linger in advance of this low with\nchances for showers or a stray thunderstorm continuing mostly in\nthe afternoon due to daytime heating. Winds will turn more \nsoutherly at 5-15 mph on Monday in response to the approaching \nthen strengthen some on Tuesday as the low draws closer to \nArizona. High temperatures will warm a bit as southerly flow \npushes a mild air mass across Arizona.\n\nFrom Wednesday onward...Models remain consistent on pushing the \nPacific low across Arizona tapping into moisture of subtropical \norigins. The result, widespread showers and likely a few \nthunderstorms, especially on Wednesday and Thursday. The most \nsubstantial moisture, with origins from over the Gulf of \nCalifornia and northwest Mexico, will likely move across Arizona \nfrom about Flagstaff eastward where local rainfall totals of 1 to\n2 inches will be possible. The area of the highest rainfall \ntotals includes the White Mountains, eastern Mogollon Rim and \nNavajo Nation. Further west looking at amounts of 0.25-0.75 \ninches. Once again the snow level will remain elevated for much of\nthe storm due to the subtropical nature of the moisture, perhaps \ndropping as low as 8500 feet as the low is exiting Arizona next \nSaturday. Pressure gradients will strengthen with breezy south to \nsouthwest winds developing. High temperatures will be on the \ndecrease under the influence of perception, clouds and cooler air \nassociated with the low.",
